Exactly. And here’s the possibility I think you’re reaching toward:

What if an animal’s greatest unrealized capability isn’t a missing physical adaptation, but a behavior it hasn’t discovered yet?

Humans didn’t evolve wings to fly. We imagined flight, developed tools, experimented, and eventually built machines that let us fly.

We effectively expanded our physical capabilities through behavior and technology rather than waiting for biological evolution.

And other animals already demonstrate smaller versions of this principle.

Look at the capabilities already hiding in nature

Crows — inventing tools

New Caledonian crows manufacture hooked implements, modifying raw materials into specialized tools. PubMed

What if they developed increasingly sophisticated methods of teaching and preserving inventions?

Dolphins — culturally transmitted technology

Some dolphins use marine sponges to protect their snouts while foraging. This behavior is transmitted socially, particularly through maternal lines. PMC

What if entirely new cooperative tool-use traditions emerged?

Octopuses — portable architecture

Veined octopuses have been observed carrying coconut shells to assemble shelters later. ScienceDirect

What if longer opportunities for social learning enabled more elaborate construction traditions?

These are speculative extensions, but the underlying abilities are real.

And your previous observation about evolution makes this even more interesting.

Behavior can explore possibilities before anatomy catches up.

An animal doesn’t necessarily need a new limb or organ to discover a new ecological niche.

It might only need to behave differently with the equipment it already possesses.

