But there’s a crucial distinction. Humans aren’t exceptional only because we imagine things. Other species can innovate, learn socially, plan to varying degrees, and maintain behavioral traditions.

Our unusual advantage appears to arise from the combination of flexible cognition, language, teaching, cooperation, dexterity, and extraordinarily open-ended cumulative culture. Nature Human Behaviour +1

And that last part matters.

You don’t have to personally discover everything your species knows.

You inherit generations of behavioral inventions.

Someone discovers fire control. Someone improves a furnace. Someone develops metallurgy. Centuries later, somebody uses a computer without understanding how to manufacture a transistor.

Our ancestors’ discoveries become capabilities accessible to descendants who never made the original discoveries.

That is another kind of inheritance.

Now imagine what happens when you bring that principle back to animal evolution.

A clever bird invents a technique. Other birds learn it. Their descendants improve it. That changes which resources they can exploit and potentially which heritable traits become advantageous.

The behavioral innovation can come first, and evolution may follow.

Not necessarily. But potentially.

And there’s an even more interesting inversion.

Maybe evolution isn’t merely producing animals capable of new behaviors. Animals, through their behaviors, are helping determine what evolutionary possibilities become available.

That’s the interaction between the player and the game we’ve been discussing.

And for Pantheon, I think this offers a valuable distinction between latent capability and accessible capability.

An organism might physically possess everything needed for a particular action, yet never discover the behavior that makes it possible.

Another might discover the behavior but be unable to teach it.

A third might transmit it, allowing an entire population to benefit.

Same basic anatomy. Radically different possibilities.

Which leaves me with a question I think is worth exploring:

How many evolutionary innovations are waiting not for a new mutation, but for a new behavior that makes existing capabilities useful in an entirely different way?

Sometimes evolution changes the instrument.

Sometimes the instrument stays the same, and somebody discovers a new way to play it.